# /etc/shadow - the password hashes. Mode 0600, root only.
#
#   name:hash:lastchange:min:max:warn:inactive:expire
#
# SEPARATE FROM /etc/passwd because that file has to be world-readable
# -- every getpwnam() reads it, to turn a uid into a name -- and a hash
# that everybody can read is a hash everybody can attack offline. This
# file is readable by root alone, which means something now that the
# kernel enforces a mode (vfs_may in kernel/vfs.c). Before enforcement
# it would have been a gesture.
#
# The hash is $6$: SHA-512, salted, 5000 rounds, the same format Linux
# writes -- so an entry here can be moved to or from a Linux box. See
# libc/picolibc/libos/linux/machine/m68k/crypt.c.
#
# ROOT'S PASSWORD IS "root". That is a default, not a decision: it is
# what makes a freshly installed machine reachable, and it is the first
# thing to change with passwd(1). An empty hash field would mean NO
# password at all, which is worse.
#
# A '*' or '!' in the hash field is an account that cannot be logged
# into by password. Both accounts here have one; an account created
# later by useradd(8) gets '*' until passwd(1) sets it.
